417] [17592186148277 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27bcbb90-63f9-5635-829b-a801f3809b3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148277 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148278 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148278 #edb/ref 1061 21951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148278 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ba22f44-3353-5e3e-a64c-1e09fd59d6d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148278 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148279 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148279 #edb/ref 1061 21954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148279 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4a1265b-c056-54ef-b3c5-eaaf48e49ec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148279 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148280 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148280 #edb/ref 1061 21955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148280 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cba5c95d-7e83-5872-ae95-b6832ff486a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148280 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148281 #edb/ref 1061 22145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148281 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8aec20bb-3034-54e2-b58d-43a8fc04ec0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148281 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148743 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the planes ${\\Pi_{{1}}}: 2x -y+z=0$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}: -x + y-z=1.$ First, notice that:\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,\\:-1,\\: 1 \\rangle.$\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -1,\\: 1,\\: -1 \\rangle.$\n\nIf two distinct planes intersect each other, their intersection will always be a straight line. A direction vector $\\mathbf{v}$ for that line can be found by computing the cross product of the normal vectors of the two planes. \n\n![](images/0c/0cf233086eccae47e640d3599908572e2c3a9996dd84d861e4d659c03108105c.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148743 #edb/ref 1065 3803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148743 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e276ddb-1c14-51fd-8261-8c02eb3294f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148743 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148744 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, given two planes \n\n$$\n{\\Pi_{{1}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{1}x+b_{1}y + c_{1}z+ d_1=0 \\qquad  \\text{and} \\qquad   {\\Pi_{{2}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{2}x+b_{2}y + c_{2}z+ d_2=0,\n$$\n\n we have the following cases, regarding the positions of their normal vectors  $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2 \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- *Case 1*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\nparallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ then ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$   and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ intersect on a straight line.\n\n- *Case 2*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\parallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ but the corresponding equations of the planes are not scalar multiples of each other, then the planes are distinct and are parallel to each other.\n\n- *Case 3*:  If the corresponding coefficients of ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ are all proportional, then the planes are coincident." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148744 #edb/ref 1065 3804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148744 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "50ffbdb5-c9d0-5815-9b29-b505cde7f0fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148744 #edb/ref 1067 "Intersection of Two Planes in 3D" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148745 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a line that is given by its vector equation\n![](images/ca/ca43fd13bd65c544d66a4de2801871c2a79087fd960db44696ce6068d30c8377.svg)\n\nSince $y=2$ at every point on the line, the corresponding Cartesian equations of this line are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1, \\quad y=2.\n$$\n\nWe may therefore interpret this line as the intersection of the planes $\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1$ and $y=2$.\n\nLet's look at one more example. Given the line \n\n![](images/1a/1a97b5bcff75bc7306d14a8af3fb1f169a2e7bf865be2e7a9c6d2741eecc63a6.svg)\n\nwe can write its Cartesian equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-2}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}2 = \\dfrac{z-1}3\n$$\n\nwhich can be further written as the system of two equations:\n\n![](images/1f/1f4cce850809f3fda876dd96e1cf9c65fd873df97bbba44e291b611896c86ca4.svg)\n\nSo, again, our line can be represented as the intersection of the planes $2x+5y-9=0$ and $3y-2z-1=0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148745 #edb/ref 1065 3817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148745 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7303bab-ef2c-5a9b-9caa-dc6fcc7929d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148745 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cartesian Equation of a Line as the Intersection of Two Planes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260264 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260264 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260264 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2bbb0f75-5837-5fef-9156-1bb0626a6799" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260355 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260355 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260355 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "358aeb92-2af7-5b63-a3fc-98a16739711a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260517 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ae1bb4f-6418-5cbe-8748-f187b44dbec8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261019 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "83b18a92-b695-5c83-b721-b2dee329f376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261817 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261817 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261817 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db99d772-027a-5c17-b647-51f20577ed39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186260517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3e5261fb-a30e-5c21-b272-d448a6432184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262998 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262998 #edb/ref 1071 "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad00dc96-c374-5320-be1d-ca73d7e96f79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262998 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336542 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "36ce110a-db9f-5911-9515-94540ed6dd91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336631 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336631 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336631 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f8de119-9259-50a9-9782-4e6da9be6936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336739 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336739 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336739 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c1db453-ad2d-5f60-9a15-9c2d0dec589f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338060 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338060 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338060 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e8227368-6969-5434-8958-753fda8ed7e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338170 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338170 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338170 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f4bff34c-6563-5a40-bee7-7ecfa61e666b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186338060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186338170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338515 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338515 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338515 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186338170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338515 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8415a7da-95f3-5c4b-9085-9ccf7a05fb9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338949 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338949 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338949 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338949 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "894f2787-9676-52de-a718-3b82f8fd7a1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338949 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413738 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413738 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413738 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0adea858-c23c-564c-9506-9e4a60396c0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413739 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413739 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413739 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22b5f065-43d8-5efe-9322-eca1974cc3d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413740 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413740 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413740 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d9614ef-645c-5eb3-83fc-f33c2f35aca1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413741 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413741 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413741 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6dc4adca-656f-5e68-9890-f48be898ac6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413742 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413742 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413742 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f5604891-08a1-55fe-b657-ffce04ef2384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413743 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413743 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413743 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413743 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "aef76bea-df64-529b-85e7-fa51fb7e165e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413744 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413744 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413744 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413744 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413744 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413744 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
